o help us he says but even if  they don't we will do everything we can  to protect our home  air raid sirens now blare almost  non-stop in the neighboring city of  kramatorsk three-quarters of its two  hundred thousand residents have fled but  some can't albino vona morova lives  alone in this mostly abandoned blown out  apartment building  i can't move i have no money she says i  grew up in russia there are brothers why  would they do this for those left behind  volunteers are providing food  before the war katarina onoshenka was a  lawyer i'm a fighter  so  i stay here and i fight for people's  life a couple of months ago she was  handing out food at this local train  station ten minutes after she left an  explosion it's terrible sound she  returned to find this a russian cluster  bomb hit the crowded station 59 people  were killed onoshenko took this photo of  the russian rocket bearing the words for  the children i don't know why  what children they mean  what about  i don't know  for my children no i don't know the  mayor says many here used to be  pro-russian not anymore now it's not not  thinkable he told global news the war is  at a critical crossroads the next two  weeks are very important for ukraine it  will depend for us first of all how long  can we hold  this line the answer could determine the  fate of the donbass and perhaps even the  war  jeff temple global news sloviensk  [Music]  the wrath of agatha ahead the deadly  hurricane wreaking havoc on southern  mexico  [Music]  in southern mexico 11 people are dead  and dozens are missing in the wake of  hurricane agatha  the storm triggered flooding and  landslides across the state of oaxaca  most of the victims were swept away by  rivers or buried in mudslides agatha  made landfall monday as a category two  hurricane one of the strongest may  storms ever to hit mexico's pacific  coast the atlantic hurricane season  begins today forecasters are predicting  above normal activity with as many as 21  named storms  scientists believe they found the  biggest plant in the world but it's  probably not what you're picturing  researchers discovered this underwater  meadow of sea grass called poseidon's  ribbon weed they found it in shark bay a  world heritage site in western australia  initially the meadow was thought to be a  cluster of thousands of individual  species but scientists now think it's  all just one genetically identical plant  that's at least 4 500 years old the  grass covers more than 180 square  kilometers  next britain on the eve of celebration  for the monarch's historic milestone  royal fans are converging on london  where final preparations are underway  for the four-day celebration of queen  elizabeth ii platinum jubilee tonight  the royal family has unveiled a new  portrait to mark her 70 years on the  throne  given the queen's age and her condition  lately one of the big questions is how  much the monarch will be seen during  these events our donna friesen is in  london on the eve of the jubilee donna  nathu it's probably safe to say no 96  year old has ever had a birthday  celebration like this queen elizabeth  has ruled longer than any british  monarch and plans are in place to  celebrate her 70-year reign in style  over an extended holiday weekend here  from tourist hot spots to buckingham  palace the stage is set to celebrate a  royal milestone and the queen's super  fans are prepared to camp out all night  to get a front row seat she deserves  this she deserves us to celebrate what a  wonderful 70 years she's given to us  this celebration is our thank you to her  it was 70 years ago that queen elizabeth  ascended to the throne after her father  died  now the final rehearsals are over led by  her grandson prince william to mark a  lifetime of service they didn't used to  have platinum jubilees quite simply  because monarchs didn't live this long  katie nichol is a journalist and author  of the new royals and i think what we've  seen during the queen's reign is that  unstinting devotion to duty mobility  issues according to the palace means  she's cut back on public events but this  is a job for life we are expecting to  see the queen on the balcony with some  members of her family do you think we'll  see much more of her i'm told by  buckingham palace that it really is  going to be a case of decisions being  made on the day what's certain is there  will be no repeat of this  only working royals will appear on the  balcony of buckingham palace not prince  andrew and not harry and megan though  they are here for the weekend do you  think there will be family drama with  their visit well i can tell you that the  instruction from the very top and that  is hmq is that no family dramas or  tensions are to overshadow any part of  this jubilee she has devoted supporters  who love her and who know this moment  is likely not going to be repeated look  there's no doubt that  a monarch that reigns  that long  pretty successfully  and has that much affection you know a  bond with her subjects here and around  the world is it is an almost impossible  act to follow  but i think this is a moment for people  to take stock of what she's achieved and  say thank you  not everyone of course will be  celebrating there are critics who say  this is just a display of colonial  nostalgia  yet there are people who can separate  their fondness and affection for the  queen from their feelings about the  institution of the monarchy itself and  tens of thousands of people are expected  to line the route for her birthday  parade tomorrow  nifu  thanks donna and you can join donna  tomorrow when she'll bring you global  national from london  but that's global national for this  wednesday night i'm nitu garcia  tonight's your canada is the springtime  shot of the downtown edmonton skyline  and the walter dale bridge thanks for  watching and have a great night  [Music]  you
 

